[00:00] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Kia EV9 connected to a travel trailer via a blue power cable. Driving shot from inside the Kia EV9 on a scenic road.
AUDIO: 'On our last camping trip, I left our noisy and heavy generator at home and instead I powered our travel trailer directly from my car, an allect electric SUV. Did it work? I\'ll tell you all you need to know in this video. My name is Scott and this is my channel, Scott'

[00:12] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: The travel trailer parked with an awning extended, and a yellow generator visible nearby.
AUDIO: 'Smart Home. Our favorite campground is in the Spring Mountains at an elevation of almost 9,000 ft. Because of its remote location, there is no electricity at this site. So, in the past, we\'ve always taken our generator with us.'

[00:24] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: Interior view of the travel trailer, showing the bed, kitchen sink, and microwave.
AUDIO: 'Generators are allowed except during quiet hours. Because it\'s so noisy, I try and keep that usage to an absolute. We only run ours when we absolutely need to. For us, that means when we\'re'

[00:37] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: The Kia EV9 parked in a desert landscape with a winding road sign.
AUDIO: 'boiling the kettle, making coffee, using the toaster or the microwave. After owning our last car, a Jeep Cherokee, for over 8 years, it was time for a new one. And I really wanted to get back into an EV. The one I bought is a Kia'

[00:50] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Close-up of the Kia EV9's V2L adapter port.
AUDIO: 'EV9, an amazing three row SUV which can tow up to £5,000, but also comes with the ability as standard to plug in external electrical devices and run those from the car\'s high voltage'

[01:02] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: The Kia EV9 with the travel trailer attached, parked in a lot with other RVs.
AUDIO: 'battery. This is known as a V2L or vehicle to load. The EV9 has a gigantic 99 kWh battery, the equivalent of over seven Tesla Power Walls. I have just two of these at home, but my car has more'

[01:16] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: The Kia EV9 towing the travel trailer on a residential street.
AUDIO: 'than three times that capacity. That\'s a lot of power. So, we left the generator behind and headed up into the mountains. So, how does it work? This car has two regular electrical outlets you can use. One is in the trunk, and one is'

[01:30] [scene:on-screen text]
VISUAL: Text overlay "15 AMPS IS EQUIVALENT TO 1800 WATTS OR 1.8 KW" over a shot of the RV.
AUDIO: 'available with this adapter that plugs into the car\'s charging port, which is the one I used. You simply plug it in, press a button, and the power starts to flow. The power available in each outlet is rated at up to 15 amps. Now, my trailer has a 30 amp plug on it, so of'

[01:44] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Close-up of the V2L adapter with its cover open, showing the electrical outlet and specifications.
AUDIO: 'course I did need to use an adapter. This is no different when you plug in uh your trailer at home. The 15 amps is a lot less than 30, right? So, is it safe? It\'s very safe because the car monitors'

[01:57] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: Interior shot of the RV kitchen area, focusing on the microwave and refrigerator.
AUDIO: 'the power draw and if you do go over the maximum 1.8 kW allowed, it will just stop the power. But is 15 amps enough to power a trailer? In my case, yes, absolutely. That is the equivalent of around 1,800 W. With just lights on'

[02:10] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: Wider interior shot of the RV, showing the kitchen, dining area, and bed.
AUDIO: 'inside, we use less than 100 W. The fridge is also running. That takes it up to around 4 to 500 W. The device with the highest power we have is our microwave, but even that and the fridge'

[02:22] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: Another interior shot of the RV kitchen, showing various items on the counter.
AUDIO: 'and the lights, that\'s 1,700 W in total. Now, there\'s other things like the coffee maker, which uses 700 W, or the toaster, which uses 800 W, the air conditioning, which uses,00 W. No'

[02:34] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: The RV interior with the kitchen and dining area visible. Side view of the Kia EV9 with the blue power cable connected to the V2L port.
AUDIO: 'problem to that running, plus the fridge and the lights, but you couldn\'t run another high power item at the same time. We just have a very simple rule. Since we know the maximum background usage is about 500 W fridge and the lights, then we just run one other high'

[02:48] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Car display showing "Transferring V2L...", "Time Remaining 27h 19m", and battery at "49%".
AUDIO: 'power device at a time. That means you definitely won\'t trip the breaker. I really enjoyed not having to drag the generator up the mountain, but also enjoying the extra peace and quiet. So, is it practical? Actually, yes, it is.'

[03:02] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Car display showing "Transferring V2L...", "Time Remaining 5h 11m", and battery at "25%".
AUDIO: 'Uh, we arrived with 49% remaining on the battery and 2 days later we had 25% left. One thing to note though is that Kia, the manufacturer, has set a minimum limit of 20%. So when you reach that,'

